- Serum glucose (to rule out hypoglycemia — a common, life-threatening cause of acute agitation);  
- Basic metabolic panel (to assess sodium, calcium, renal function — hyponatremia and uremia cause delirium);  
- Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) — hyperthyroidism can present with acute agitation, especially in older adults;  
- Urine toxicology screen (to detect stimulants, anticholinergics, or other intoxicants).  
Of these, glucose is fastest, cheapest, and most emergently actionable. Hypoglycemia can cause agitation before overt neuroglycopenic signs and is easily missed without measurement.

